Tearing a strip off the plane | 1998 | J. Graph Theory 29, No. 1, 17-33 (1998).
  [ summary | None ] Summary: We examine some homomorphic properties of certain subgraphs of the unit-distance graph. We define \(G_r\) to be the subgraph of the unit-distance graph induced by the subset \((-\infty,\infty)\times [0,r]\) of the plane. The bulk of the article is devoted to examining the graphs \(G_r\), when \(r\) is the minimum width such that \(G_r\) contains an odd cycle of given length. We determine for each odd \(n\) the minimum width \(r_n\) such that \(G_{r_n}\) contains an \(n\)-cycle \(C_n\), and characterize the embeddings of \(C_n\) in \(G_{r_n}\). We then show that \(G_{r_n}\) is homomorphically equivalent to \(C_n\) when \(n\equiv 3\pmod 4\), but \(G_{r_n}\) is a core when \(n\equiv 1\pmod 4\). We begin by showing that \(G_r\) is homomorphically compact for each \(r\geq 0\), as defined in [\textit{B. L. Bauslaugh}, Cores and compactness in infinite directed graphs, J. Comb. Theory, Ser. B 68, No. 2, 255-276, Art. No. 0068 (1996; Zbl 0861.05028)]. We conclude with some other interesting results and open problems related to the graphs \(G_r\).
Coloring unit-distance strips using SAT | 2020 | Albert, Elvira (ed.) et al., LPAR-23. 23rd international conference on logic for programming, artificial intelligence and reasoning, Alicante, Spain, May 22--27, 2020. Selected papers. Manchester: EasyChair. EPiC Ser. Comput. 73, 373-389 (2020).
  [ summary | None ] Summary: Satisfiability (SAT) solving has become an important technology in computer-aided mathematics with various successes in number and graph theory. In this paper we apply SAT solvers to color infinitely long strips in the plane with a given height and number of colors. The coloring is constrained as follows: two points that are exactly unit distance apart must be colored differently. To finitize the problem, we tile the strips and all points on a tile have the same color. We evaluated our approach using two different tile shapes: squares and hexagons. The visualization of bounded height strips using 3 to 6 colors reveal patterns that are similar to the best known lower bounds for infinite strips. Our method can be a useful tool for mathematicians to search for patterns that can be generalized to infinite strips and allowed us to increase the lower bound for the strip height with 5 colors to an improved height of 1.700084.

For the entire collection see [Zbl 1545.68007].
